      "math_academy_id": "q-164275",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-4$ and $z=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4).$",
